      <p class="lead-text">
        Every year, more than 4 million Americans turn 65 and confront the most consequential healthcare financing choice of their retirement: Should they enroll in Original Medicare with a Medigap supplemental policy, or hand their Medicare benefits to a private Medicare Advantage plan (Medicare Part C)?
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison: Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap (Plan G & Plan N)</h2>
      <p>
        The table below provides a side-by-side architectural comparison of both paths under current 2026 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) fee schedules:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Evaluation Feature</th>
              <th>Medicare Advantage (Part C)</th>
              <th>Medigap Plan G</th>
              <th>Medigap Plan N</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Monthly Premium</strong></td>
