      <h2>Understanding Medicare Advantage (Medicare Part C)</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare Advantage plans are managed care programs administered by private health insurers (such as UnitedHealthcare, Humana, CVS/Aetna, and Kaiser Permanente). When you enroll in an Advantage plan, the federal government pays the private insurer a fixed monthly capitation fee to manage your healthcare risk.
      </p>

      <h3>The Advantages of Part C</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Low or Zero Additional Premium:</strong> Many counties offer plans with a $0 monthly premium beyond your mandatory Medicare Part B premium.</li>
        <li><strong>All-in-One Convenience:</strong> Medical coverage, prescription drugs, and minor dental or eyewear stipends are bundled onto a single member ID card.</li>
        <li><strong>Financial Out-of-Pocket Ceiling:</strong> Unlike traditional Original Medicare (which has no catastrophic spending cap), Advantage plans cap your annual in-network hospital and doctor expenses at a maximum of $8,850.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The Serious Drawbacks & Hidden Costs of Part C</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Prior Authorization Delays:</strong> Independent studies by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of Inspector General revealed that private Medicare Advantage plans inappropriately denied tens of thousands of prior authorization requests that met Original Medicare coverage rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Restricted Geographic Networks:</strong> If you travel frequently or spend winters in Florida or Arizona, an HMO Advantage plan may not cover routine care outside your home county.</li>
        <li><strong>Accumulating Copayments:</strong> While monthly premiums may be $0, copays add up rapidly if you require intensive therapy, chemotherapy, or extended skilled nursing stays ($300–$400/day hospital copays for the first 5 to 7 days).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Understanding Medicare Supplement Insurance (Medigap)</h2>
      <p>
        Medigap policies are standardized secondary insurance contracts designed to fill the financial "gaps" in Original Medicare—specifically the 20% coinsurance on Part B doctor services and Part A inpatient hospital deductibles ($1,676 in 2026).
      </p>

      <h3>Why Medigap Plan G Is the Gold Standard</h3>
      <p>
        Since Plan F was closed to new Medicare beneficiaries who became eligible after January 1, 2020, <strong>Plan G</strong> has become the premier choice for retirement security. Plan G covers 100% of the Medicare-approved gaps, leaving you with only one out-of-pocket obligation: the annual Part B deductible ($257). Once that deductible is satisfied in January or February, your medical care for the remainder of the calendar year is paid at 100%.
      </p>

      <h3>Medigap Plan N: The Lower-Cost Alternative</h3>
      <p>
        For seniors seeking complete doctor freedom but unwilling to pay Plan G's higher premiums, <strong>Plan N</strong> offers premiums 25% to 35% lower. In exchange, Plan N policyholders agree to pay:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A copayment of up to $20 for physician office visits;</li>
        <li>A copayment of up to $50 for emergency room visits that do not lead to an inpatient admission;</li>
        <li>Excess charges (rare doctor surcharges permitted in states that don't ban balance billing).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Critical Medigap Guaranteed Issue Window</h2>
      <p>
        This is the most critical rule in Medicare retirement planning: Your <strong>Medigap Open Enrollment Period</strong> lasts for precisely <strong>six months</strong>, starting on the first day of the month you are both age 65 or older AND enrolled in Medicare Part B.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ The Medical Underwriting Trap</div>
        <p class="callout-text">
          During your 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment window, insurance companies are legally prohibited from denying you a policy or charging higher premiums based on existing cancer, diabetes, cardiac conditions, or rheumatoid arthritis. Once this window closes, in 46 states insurers can review your medical records, charge exorbitant surcharges, or reject your application outright.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>How to Decide: 4 Questions to Find Your Path</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Do you travel or split time between two residences?</strong> If yes, Medigap is almost always the safer choice because it allows you to visit any Medicare-accepting hospital in all 50 states without network penalties.</li>
        <li><strong>Do you have preferred doctors or specialized cancer centers?</strong> Specialized institutions (such as Memorial Sloan Kettering or MD Anderson) often refuse Medicare Advantage HMO networks, but they accept Original Medicare and Medigap.</li>
        <li><strong>What is your monthly discretionary budget?</strong> If an extra $150–$200/month would severely strain your fixed income, Medicare Advantage provides an affordable entry point with routine perks.</li>
        <li><strong>What is your tolerance for administrative denials?</strong> If having your doctor's treatment recommendations subject to third-party insurance review causes you anxiety, Medigap provides complete clinical freedom.</li>
      </ol>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Medicare Plans</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can I switch from Medicare Advantage back to Medigap later? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            You can drop Medicare Advantage during the Annual Enrollment Period (Oct 15 – Dec 7) or Medicare Advantage Open Enrollment (Jan 1 – Mar 31) and return to Original Medicare. However, unless you qualify for a federal "guaranteed issue right" (or reside in states with continuous open enrollment like CT, NY, ME, or CA birthday rules), private Medigap carriers will require medical underwriting and can deny your application.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Does Medigap cover dental, vision, and hearing? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. Standard Medigap policies (including Plans A through N) do not cover routine dental cleanings, dentures, eyeglasses, or hearing aids. Beneficiaries on Medigap who desire dental and vision coverage typically enroll in separate standalone private dental/vision policies or join discount dental programs.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the Medicare Part B premium for 2026? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The standard Part B premium is projected at approximately $185 per month. High-income beneficiaries with modified adjusted gross income exceeding $106,000 (individual) or $212,000 (married filing jointly) pay an Income-Related Monthly Adjustment Amount (IRMAA) surcharge on both Part B and Part D.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

